{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1502197496,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMDk0OTcxOQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1691#issuecomment-320949719"},"message":"I think it should not even attempt to publish something that does not exist locally. it would be more or less incomprehensible to error on something that is invisible to a user. `publish -r` should only recursive into installed datasets. I consider this a bug the refactored `publish` that now uses path annotation. So it should be fixed in `dev-master`.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1502273574,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMTIxMzk3OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1692#issuecomment-321213979"},"message":"Tried to build a minimal case. Empty dataset (`datalad create` in an empty dir).\n`--on-failure=ignore` works for `get`, but doesn't for `publish`, which still returns non-zero:\n\n```\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % datalad get non-existent \nget(impossible): /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE/non-existent [path does not exist]\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % echo $?\n1\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % datalad --on-failure=ignore get non-existent\nget(impossible): /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE/non-existent [path does not exist]\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % echo $?\n0\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % datalad publish --to=non-existent\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % echo $?\n1\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % datalad --on-failure=ignore publish --to=non-existent\nben@tree /tmp/TEST_ON_FAILURE % echo $?\n1\n```","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"819663238374c863923453c36b916c251f565216"},"timestamp":1502273870,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDMyMTIxNDk2MQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1692#issuecomment-321214961"},"message":"FTR: This is on dev-master.","file